+# arXiv 1207.0255 — fixed-path interval-extension generator
+
+| Profile | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | exact cover |
+| Certificate | matrix certificate |
+| Objects shown | fixed host path, partially represented interval graph, pre-drawn singleton subpaths, unlocated path components |
+| Intended intuition | invariant: three size bands carry affine copies of one residue-tag set |
+| Domain essentiality | **native** |
+| Reduction | none in the solver task; instances use the paper's own Theorem 3 gadgets |
+
+## What the problem is, and whether to trust it
+
+The source is Klavik, Kratochvil, Otachi and Saitoh, [*Extending Partial
+Representations of Subclasses of Chordal Graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.0255).
+The solver receives exactly the objects in the paper's `RepExt(INT, Fixed)`
+problem: a fixed path, singleton subpaths already assigned to some graph
+vertices, and unlocated path components. It returns a `3n × 2` matrix. Row
+`i = [g,r]` puts take component `T_i` in gap `g` at left-to-right rank `r`.
+The checker expands that row into the canonical closed-subpath representation
+of the entire path component and checks exact gap capacities and boundaries.
+No floating point, search, planted-answer comparison, or theorem oracle occurs
+in `verify`.
+
+Local correctness evidence is complete: all nine gates pass, including 12/12
+planted checks, 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses, four attacks at 0/8, a
+successful Track-B reference at 8/8, and 80 relabelling/reflection checks. The
+required external four-vendor hardening verdict is **not complete**: on
+2026-09-05 the repository-provided OpenRouter key returned HTTP 403 “Key limit
+exceeded (total limit)” on every redraw. The script-owned error transcript is
+preserved; it is not presented as model failure.
+
+## Why this is Track B
+
+Section 1.2 defines the four host-tree modification types; this family uses
+`Fixed`. Section 2 proves that connected components occupy disjoint areas and
+distinguishes located from unlocated components. Lemma 6 gives
+`minspan(P_A)=A`. In Section 3.3, Theorem 3 places split singletons at
+`p_(M+1)j`, leaving `n` gaps of exactly `M` vertices, and represents every
+3-PARTITION item of size `A_i` by an unlocated take component `P_Ai`. An
+extension exists exactly when three take components fill every gap.
+
+The general problem is NP-complete, but that is not a distributional hardness
+claim. This generator therefore does not claim Track A. Its generated lengths
+have a promised affine residue encoding. The disclosed reference algorithm
+forms every low-band/middle-band pair and then applies the affine selector. It
+is `O(n²)` on this distribution, succeeds 8/8, and at shipping examines 1,764
+cross-band pairs and performs 2,060 counted exact operations per instance
+(0.006174 s total for eight local instances). The compact route derives
+`q²=M/12`, reduces the three bands modulo `q`, and aligns their common tags in
+at most 254 exact operations. The benchmark asks whether a no-tool solver sees
+that invariant instead of carrying out the larger exact-pair table.
+
+The easy regimes matter. `Recog*(INT,Fixed)` with no partial representation is
+linear by Proposition 1. `RepExt(INT,Sub)` is linear by Theorem 2. Section 3.4
+notes that making every component located restores a fixed ordering and a
+linear test, and Proposition 5 makes bounded host-path size FPT. The generator
+keeps many unlocated components, a growing path, pre-drawn splitters, and the
+fixed modification type.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+This is the complete non-hint output of `render(make_instance(seed=7,
+**DIFFICULTY["demo"]))`:
+
+```text
+Extend this partial interval representation in its fixed host path.
+
+Definitions.
+A subpath [a,b] is the nonempty set of host vertices p_a,p_(a+1),...,p_b, with integer endpoints 0 <= a <= b.
+An interval representation assigns one subpath to every graph vertex; two distinct graph vertices must be adjacent exactly when their assigned subpaths intersect.
+The host path is fixed: it may not be subdivided and no vertices or branches may be added.
+
+Here n=2 and M=122412. The fixed host path has vertices p_0 through p_244826.
+The graph is the disjoint union of split vertices s_0,...,s_2 and 6 take components T_0,...,T_5.
+Only split vertices are pre-drawn: s_j is the singleton subpath [122413*j,122413*j] for every j=0,...,2.
+Take component T_i is a path with A_i edges and labelled graph vertices x_(i,0),...,x_(i,A_i); its A_i is listed below. No vertex of a take component is pre-drawn.
+Thus gap g is the M host vertices strictly between s_g and s_(g+1), namely coordinates (M+1)g+1 through (M+1)g+M, inclusive.
+
+Output a compact placement matrix with one row [g,r] for each T_i, in increasing i order. Here g is its 0-based gap and r is its 0-based left-to-right rank in that gap.
+Every gap must use ranks 0,1,2 exactly once. The checker packs those three components without empty coordinates, in rank order.
+If a component with A edges starts at coordinate h, the checker assigns x_(i,0)=[h,h], x_(i,A)=[h+A-1,h+A-1], and each x_(i,j)=[h+j-1,h+j] for 0<j<A.
+This is a full, deterministic interval representation: the three component areas must fill their gap exactly, may not touch a split coordinate, and areas in different components are disjoint.
+
+Component sizes, as i: A_i:
+0: 50498
+1: 30907
+2: 41007
+3: 30704
+4: 50601
+5: 41107
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as a JSON array of exactly 6 two-integer rows [gap,rank], in T_i order.
+Example: <answer>[[0,1],[1,0],[0,2],[0,0],[1,2],[1,1]]</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+One answer is:
+
+```json
+[[1,2],[1,0],[1,1],[0,0],[0,2],[0,1]]
+```
+
+`verify` returns `(True, "ok")`. Swapping `T_0` and `T_3` between their gaps
+gives `[[0,0],[1,0],[1,1],[1,2],[0,2],[0,1]]`; it returns
+`(False, "capacity mismatch: gap 0 receives total span 142206, not M=122412")`.
+A person can solve this demo on paper: there are only six lengths and two
+three-item sums to find.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | Bins `n` | Take components | `q` | Residue-blind restarts screened | Ships? |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 2 | 6 | 101 | 0 | no; hand example |
+| easy | 18 | 54 | 1009 | 64 | no |
+| medium | 30 | 90 | 1009 | 128 | no |
+| hard | 42 | 126 | 1009 | 256 | **yes** |
+
+Difficulty grows through the number of simultaneously matched components. At
+the shipping atom limit, `escalate` first raises the modulus and attack-screen
+budget while leaving the 252-atom certificate unchanged.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Result | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 preset/seed plants verify and JSON-round-trip |
+| G2 | pass | seven corruptions rejected with seven distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged fenced JSON with surrounding prose round-trips; garbage returns `None` |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware candidates; declared space about `1.33e186` |
+| G5 | pass | shipping density 0/200,000; demo 72/288 placements; strongest failing attack 157,350 operations and 0.077539 s over eight instances |
+| G6 | pass | four attacks each 0/8; disclosed reference 8/8, 16,480 operations and 0.006174 s total |
+| G7 | pass | candidate space rises at every rung; doubled `n=84` instance builds and verifies; fixed-length escalation raises `q` |
+| G8 | pass | 80/80 key-invariance and carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 853 characters, 214 estimated tokens, 252 atoms, 254 intended exact operations |
+
+The G6 attacks are size-band rank zipping, largest-first exact pairing, 256
+random residue-blind exact-pair restarts, and the in-context “sort and take
+adjacent triples” heuristic. The successful quadratic algorithm is correctly
+under `reference_algorithm`, not misreported as a failed attack.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+The bare harness began at `easy`, as required, but no oracle call was billable:
+
+| Preset | Seed | Model | Outcome | Why |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| easy | 1565752025 | OpenAI GPT-5.6 Terra | error | HTTP 403 key total limit |
+| easy | 363956064 | Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | error | HTTP 403 key total limit |
+| easy | 167874895 | OpenAI GPT-5.6 Terra | error | HTTP 403 key total limit |
+| easy | 697063186 | OpenAI GPT-5.6 Terra | error | HTTP 403 key total limit; harness stopped |
+
+| G9 arm | Solved / attempts | Status |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0 / 0 completed | blocked by OpenRouter key limit |
+| structural hint | 0 / 0 completed | not run; same external blocker |
+| placebo hint | 0 / 0 completed | not run; same external blocker |
+
+Consequently hinted-minus-placebo is not yet a meaningful measurement. Once
+quota is restored, rerun each arm in its own scratch directory; do not treat
+the existing API errors as unsolved model attempts.
+
+## How to use it
+
+From the repository root:
+
+```python
+import importlib.util
+
+path = "results/1207.0255/gen_1207_0255.py"
+spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("g", path)
+g = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
+spec.loader.exec_module(g)
+
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY["demo"])
+text = g.render(inst)
+candidate = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + __import__("json").dumps(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>")
+assert g.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+Emit samples with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1207.0255 20
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This is intentionally not a complexity-theoretic hard distribution. A tool
+that computes residues, or the disclosed `O(n²)` reference, makes it easy; a
+structural hint may also make it easy, which would be useful G9 evidence rather
+than a rejection. The 0/200,000 estimate is only for the uniform prior that
+already assigns one member of each visibly forced size band to every labeled
+gap and randomizes ranks. It is not an upper bound against informed modular,
+SAT, ILP, CP-SAT, or learned proposals.
+
+No external SAT/ILP/CP solver or long local search was run. The strongest local
+residue-blind attack is capped at 256 restarts, and its 0/8 result says nothing
+about more restarts. The compact certificate describes a deterministic full
+representation rather than spelling out millions of individual vertex
+subpaths; the checker validates that expansion symbolically from the exact path
+formula. Finally, the missing oracle and G9 runs are a real release blocker,
+not evidence of hardness; rerun `harden.py` with a funded OpenRouter key before
+submission.
