   120	\heading{Clique Orderings of \boldint.}
   121	Recall the properties of maximal cliques from Section~\ref{sec:prelim}. For a component $C$, we denote
   122	by $\cl(C)$ the number of maximal cliques of $C$. Let $\calR$ be a representation of $C$. Since the
   123	subtrees $R_K$ corresponding to the maximal cliques are pairwise disjoint, they have to be ordered
   124	from left to right. This ordering has the following well-known property~\cite{maximal_cliques}:
   125	
   126	\begin{lemma}[Fulkerson and Gross] \label{interval_char}
   127	A graph is an interval graph if and only if there exists an ordering of the maximal cliques $K_1 <
   128	\cdots < K_{\cl(C)}$ such that for each vertex $v$ the cliques containing $v$ appear consecutively
   129	in this ordering.
   130	\end{lemma}
   131	
   132	We quickly argue about the correctness of the lemma.  Clearly, in an interval representation, all
   133	maximal cliques containing one vertex $v$ appear consecutively. (Otherwise the clique in between
   134	would be intersected by $R_v$ in addition.) On the other hand, having an ordering $<$ of the maximal
   135	cliques from the statement, we can construct a representation as follows.  Assign a vertex $p_i$ of
   136	$T$ to each clique $K_i$, respecting the ordering $<$.  For each vertex $v$, we assign $R_v = \{p_i
   137	\mid v \in K_i\}$.  Since the maximal cliques containing $v$ appear consecutively, each $R_v$ is a
   138	subpath.
   139	
   140	\heading{Minimum Spans of \boldint.}
   141	We again consider the minimum span defined exactly as for proper interval
   142	graphs above. Clearly, $\minspan(C) \ge \cl(C)$. We show:
   143	
   144	\begin{lemma} \label{lem:int_minspan}
   145	For an unlocated component $C$ of an interval graph, $\minspan(C) = \cl(C)$.  We can find a smallest
   146	representation in time $\O(n+m)$.
   147	\end{lemma}
   148	
   149	\begin{proof}
   150	We start by identifying maximal cliques in time $\O(n+m)$, using the algorithm of Rose et
   151	al.~\cite{recog_chordal_graphs}. To construct a smallest representation, we find an ordering from
   152	Lemma~\ref{interval_char}, using the PQ-tree algorithm~\cite{PQ_trees} in time $\O(n+m)$. If such
   153	an ordering does not exist, the graph $G$ is not an interval graph and no representation exists. If the
   154	ordering exists, we can construct a representation using exactly $\cl(C)$ vertices of the path as
   155	described above, by putting $R_v = \{p_i \mid v \in K_i\}$.\qed
   156	\end{proof}
   157	
   158	We note that this approach does not translate to located components, as in
   159	Lemma~\ref{lem:pint_minspan} for proper interval graphs. We prove in Corollary~\ref{cor:int_add_npc}
   160	that finding the minimum span for a located component is an \cNP-complete problem. (We prove this in the
   161	setting that the problem $\ext(\int,\add)$ is \cNP-complete. In the reduction, we ask whether a
   162	connected interval graph has the minimum span at most $(M+1)k+1$ for some integers $k$ and $M$.)
