   165	
   166	First we deal with all polynomial cases.
   167	
   168	\heading{Fixed Type Recognition.} We just need to use the values of minimum spans we already know
   169	how to compute.
   170	
   171	\begin{proposition} \label{prop:int_fixed}
   172	Both $\recog^*(\pint,\fixed)$ and $\recog^*(\int,\fixed)$ can be solved in time $\O(n+m)$.
   173	\end{proposition}
   174	
   175	\begin{proof}
   176	We process the components $C_1,\dots,C_c$ one-by-one and place them on $T'$ from left to right. If
   177	$\sum_{i=1}^c \minspan(C_i) \le |T'|$, we can place the components using smallest representations
   178	from Lemma~\ref{lem:pint_minspan} for \pint, resp.~Lemma~\ref{lem:int_minspan} for \int. Otherwise,
   179	the path is too small and a representation cannot be constructed.\qed
   180	\end{proof}
   181	
   182	\heading{Add Type Extension, \boldpint.}
   183	Again, we approach this problem using minimum spans and Lemma~\ref{lem:pint_minspan}.
   184	
   185	\begin{proposition} \label{prop:pint_add}
   186	The problem $\ext(\pint,\add)$ can be solved in time $\O(n+m)$.
   187	\end{proposition}
   188	
   189	\begin{proof}
   190	Since the path can be expanded to the left and to the right as much as necessary, we can place unlocated
   191	components far to the left. So we only need to deal with located components, ordered $C_1 \blt
   192	\cdots \blt C_c$ from left to right. We process the components from left to right. When we place
   193	$C_i$, it has to be placed on the right of $C_{i-1}$. We have (at most) two possible smallest representations
   194	corresponding to two different orderings of $C_i$. We test whether at least one of them can be
   195	placed on the right of $C_{i-1}$, and pick the one minimizing the right-most vertex of $T$ taken by
   196	$C_i$ (leaving the maximum possible space for $C_{i+1},\dots,C_c$). If neither of the smallest
   197	representations can be placed, the extension algorithm outputs ``no''.
   198	
   199	If the algorithm finishes, it constructs a correct representation. On the other hand, we place each
   200	component as far to the left as possible (while restricted by the previous components on the left).
   201	So if $C_i$ cannot be placed, there exists no representation extending the partial
   202	representation.\qed
   203	\end{proof}
