   205	\heading{Non-fixed Type Recognition.}
   206	The only limitation for recognition of interval graphs inside a given path is the length of the
   207	path. In the three types \sub, \add\ and \both, we can produce a path as long as necessary. (With
   208	the trivial exception $T' = P_0$ for \sub\ for which the instance is solvable if and only if $G=K_n$.)
   209	For a subpaths-in-path representation, the order of the endpoints of the subpaths from left to right
   210	is the only thing that matters, not the exact positions. In a tree $T$ with at least $2n$ vertices,
   211	every possible ordering is realizable.
   212	
   213	Thus the problems are equivalent to the standard recognition of interval graphs on the real line.
   214	The recognition can be solved in time $\O(n+m)$; see~\cite{proper_first,uint_corneil} for \pint,
   215	and~\cite{PQ_trees,LBFS_int} for \int.
   216	
   217	\heading{Both Type Extension.}
   218	This extension type is equivalent with the partial representation extension problems of interval
   219	graphs on the real line. Again only the ordering of the endpoints is important. The only change here
   220	is that some of the endpoints are already placed. By subdividing, we can place any amount of the
   221	endpoints between any two endpoints (not sharing the same position). Also, the path can be extended
   222	to the left and to the right which allows to place any amount of endpoints to the left of the
   223	left-most pre-drawn endpoint and to the right of the right-most pre-drawn endpoint. So any extending
   224	ordering can be realized in the \both\ type.
   225	
   226	The partial representation extension problem for interval graphs on the real line was first
   227	considered in~\cite{kkv}. The paper gives algorithms for both classes \int\ and \pint, and does not
   228	explicitly deal with representations sharing endpoints but the algorithms are easy to modify.
   229	The results~\cite{blas_rutter,kkosv,kkorssv} show that both extension problems are solvable in
   230	time $\O(n+m)$.
