   343	\subsection{The NP-complete Cases}
   344	
   345	The basic gadgets of the reductions are paths. They have the following minimum spans.
   346	
   347	\begin{lemma} \label{lem:path}
   348	For\/ \int, $\minspan(P_n) = n$. For\/ \pint\ and $n \ge 2$, $\minspan(P_n) = n+2$.
   349	\end{lemma}
   350	
   351	\begin{proof}
   352	For \int, the number of the maximal cliques of $P_n$ is $n$. For \pint, the ordering $\lessdot$ is
   353	$$\ell_0 \lessdot \ell_1 \lessdot r_0 \lessdot \ell_2 \lessdot r_1 \lessdot \cdots \lessdot \ell_i
   354	\lessdot r_{i-1} \lessdot \cdots \lessdot \ell_n \lessdot r_{n-1} \lessdot r_n.$$
   355	There are $n$ changes from $\ell_i$ to $r_{i-1}$ and $P_n$ has $n+1$ vertices. So the minimum span
   356	equals $2(n+1)-n=n+2$.\qed
   357	\end{proof}
   358	
   359	We reduce the problems from \partition. An input of \partition\ consists of positive integers $k$, $M$ and
   360	$A_1,\dots,A_{3k}$ such that ${M \over 4} < A_i < {M \over 2}$ for each $A_i$ and $\sum A_i = kM$.
   361	It asks whether it is possible to partition $A_i$'s into $k$ triples such that the sets $A_i$ of each
   362	triple sum to exactly $M$.\footnote{Notice that if a subset of $A_i$'s sums to exactly $M$ it has to
   363	be a triple due to the size constraints.} This problem is strongly \cNP-complete~\cite{partition}
   364	which means that it is \cNP-complete even when the input is coded in unary, i.e., all integers are
   365	of polynomial sizes.
   366	
   367	\begin{theorem} \label{thm:int_npc}
   368	The problems $\ext(\pint,\fixed)$ and $\ext(\int,\fixed)$ are \cNP-complete.
   369	\end{theorem}
   370	
   371	\begin{proof}
   372	We use almost the same reductions for both \pint\ and \int. For a given input of \partition\ (with
   373	$M \ge 4$), we construct a graph $G$ and its partial representation as follows.
   374	
   375	As the fixed tree we choose $T'=P_{(M+1)k}$, with the vertices $p_0,\dots,p_{(M+1)k}$.  The graph
   376	$G$ contains two types of gadgets as separate components. First, it contains $k+1$ \emph{split
   377	gadgets} $S_0,\dots,S_k$ which split the path into $k$ gaps of the size $M$. Then it contains
   378	$3k$ \emph{take gadgets} $T_1,\dots,T_{3k}$. A take gadget $T_i$ takes in each representation at
   379	least $A_i$ vertices of one of the $k$ gaps.
   380	
   381	For these reductions, the gadgets are particularly simple. The split gadget $S_i$ is just a single
   382	pre-drawn vertex $v_i$ with $R_{v_i} = \{p_{(M+1)i}\}$. The split gadgets clearly split the path
   383	into the $k$ gaps of the size $M$. The take gadget $T_i$ is $P_{A_i}$ for \int, resp.~$P_{A_i-2}$
   384	for \pint.  According to Lemma~\ref{lem:path}, $\minspan(T_i) = A_i$. The representation is
   385	extendible if and only if it is possible to place the take gadgets into the $k$ gaps. For an
   386	example, see Fig.~\ref{fig:int_partition}. The reduction is clearly polynomial.
   387	
   388	\begin{figure}[t]
   389	\centering
   390	\includegraphics{interval_graphs1_partition.eps}
   391	\caption{An example of the reduction for the following input of \partition: $k = 2$, $M = 7$, $A_1 =
   392	A_2 = A_3 = A_4 = 2$ and $A_5 = A_6 = 3$. On top, the constructed interval graph is depicted. On
   393	bottom, the partial representation (depicted in bold) is extended.}
   394	\label{fig:int_partition}
   395	\end{figure}
   396	
   397	To conclude the proof, we show that the partial representation is extendible if and only if the
   398	corresponding \partition\ input has a solution. If the partial representation is extendible, the
   399	take gadgets $T_i$ are divided into the $k$ gaps on the path which gives a partition. Based on the
   400	constraints for the sizes of $A_i$'s, each gap contains exactly three take gadgets of the total minimum
   401	span $M$; thus the partition solves the \partition\ problem. On the other hand, a solution of
   402	\partition\ describes how to place the take gadgets into the $k$ gaps and construct an extending
   403	representation.\qed
   404	\end{proof}
   405	
   406	\begin{corollary} \label{cor:int_add_npc}
   407	The problem $\ext(\int,\add)$ is \cNP-complete.
   408	\end{corollary}
   409	
   410	\begin{proof}
   411	We use the above reduction for \int\ with one additional pre-drawn interval $v$ attached to
   412	everything in $G$.  We put $R_v = \{p_0,\dots,p_{(M+1)k}\}$, so it contains the whole tree $T'$.
   413	Since a representation of each take gadget $T_i$ has to intersect $R_v$, it has to be placed inside
   414	of the $k$ gaps as before.\qed
   415	\end{proof}
   416	
   417	We note that the above modification does not work for proper interval graphs. Indeed, this is not
   418	very surprising since Proposition~\ref{prop:pint_add} states that the problem $\ext(\pint,\add)$ can
   419	be solved in time $\O(n+m)$.
   420	
   421	\subsection{The Parameterized Complexity} \label{sec:int_param}
   422	
   423	In this subsection, we study the parameterized complexity. The parameters are the number $c$ of components,
   424	the number $k$ of pre-drawn intervals and the size $t$ of the path $T'$.
   425	
   426	\heading{By the Number of Components.} In the reduction of Theorem~\ref{thm:int_npc}, one might ask
   427	whether it is possible to make the reduction graph $G$ connected. For \int, it is indeed possible to
   428	add a universal vertex adjacent to everything in $G$, and thus make $G$ connected as in the proof
   429	of Corollary~\ref{cor:int_add_npc}. The following result answers this question for \pint\
   430	negatively (unless $\cP=\cNP$):
   431	
   432	\begin{proposition}
   433	The problem $\ext(\pint,\fixed)$ is fixed-parameter trac\-table in the number $c$ of components,
   434	solvable in time $\O((n+m)c!)$.
   435	\end{proposition}
   436	
   437	\begin{proof}
   438	There are $c!$ possible orderings $\blt$ of the components from left to right, and we test each of
   439	them.  (The located components force some partial ordering $\blt$ so we need to test less then $c!$
   440	orderings; see below the proof for details.) We show that for a prescribed ordering $\blt$ of the
   441	components, we can solve the problem in time $\O(n+m)$; thus gaining the total time $\O((n+m)c!)$.
   442	We solve the problem almost the same as in the proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:pint_add}. The only
   443	difference is that we deal with all components instead of only the located ones.
   444	
   445	We process the components from left to right. When we process $C_i$, we place it on the right of
   446	$C_{i-1}$ as far to the left as possible. For the unlocated $C_i$, we can take any smallest
   447	representation. For the located $C_i$, we test both smallest representations and take the one placing
   448	the right-most endpoint of $C_i$ further to the left.  We construct the representation in time
   449	$\O(n+m)$. For the correctness of the algorithm see the proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:pint_add} for
   450	more details.\qed
   451	\end{proof}
   452	
   453	We note that for \cNP-hardness of the problem $\ext(\pint,\fixed)$ it is necessary to have some
   454	pre-drawn subpaths. On the other hand, also some unlocated components are necessary. If all the
   455	components were located, there would be a unique ordering $\blt$ and we could test it in time
   456	$\O(n+m)$ as described above.  In general, for $c$ components and $c'$ located components, we need
   457	to test only $c! \over c'!$ different orderings.
   458	
   459	\heading{By the Number of Pre-drawn Intervals.}
   460	In the reduction in Theorem~\ref{thm:int_npc}, we need to have $k$ pre-drawn intervals. One could
   461	ask, whether the problems become simpler with a small number of pre-drawn intervals. We answer this
   462	negatively. For \pint, the problem is in \cXP\ and \cW1-hard with respect to $k$. For \int, we
   463	only show that it is \cW1-hard.
   464	
   465	There are two closely related problems \binpack\ and \genbinpack. In both problems, we have
   466	$k$ bins and $n$ items of positive integer sizes. The question is whether we can pack (partition)
   467	these items into the $k$ bins when the volumes of the bins are limited. For \binpack, all the bins have
   468	the same volume. For \genbinpack, the bins have different volumes. Formally:
   469	
   470	\computationproblem{\binpack}
   471	{Positive integers $k$, $\ell$, $V$, and $A_1,\dots,A_\ell$.}
   472	{Does there exist a $k$-partition $\calP_1,\dots,\calP_k$ of $A_1,\dots,A_\ell$ such that $\sum_{A_i
   473	\in \calP_j} A_i \le V$ for every $\calP_j$.}
   474	
   475	\computationproblem{\genbinpack}
   476	{Positive integers $k$, $\ell$, $V_1,\dots,V_k$, and $A_1,\dots,A_\ell$.}
   477	{Does there exist a $k$-partition $\calP_1,\dots,\calP_k$ of $A_1,\dots,A_\ell$ such that $\sum_{A_i
   478	\in \calP_j} A_i \le V_j$ for every $\calP_j$.}
   479	
   480	\begin{lemma}
   481	The problems\/ \binpack\ and\/ \genbinpack\ are polynomially equivalent.
   482	\end{lemma}
   483	
   484	\begin{proof}
   485	Obviously \binpack\ is a special case of \genbinpack. On the other hand, let $k$, $\ell$,
   486	$V_1,\dots,V_k$, and $A_1,\dots,A_\ell$ be an instance of \genbinpack. We construct an instance $k'$,
   487	$\ell'$, $V'$, and $A'_1,\dots,A'_{\ell'}$ of \binpack\ as follows. We put $k'=k$, $\ell' = \ell+k$
   488	and $V' = 2\cdot\max V_i + 1$. The sizes of the first $\ell$ items are the same, i.e, $A'_i = A_i$ for
   489	$i=1,\dots,\ell$. The additional items $A'_{\ell+1},\dots,A'_{\ell+k}$ are called \emph{large} and we put
   490	$A'_{\ell+i} = V' - V_i$ for $i=1,\dots,k$.
   491	
   492	Each bin has to contain exactly one large item since two large items take more space than $V'$.
   493	After placing large items into the bins, we obtain the bins of the remaining volumes $V_1,\dots,V_k$
   494	in which we have to place the remaining items. This corresponds exactly to the original \genbinpack\
   495	instance.\qed
   496	\end{proof}
   497	
   498	If the sizes of items are encoded in binary, the problem is \cNP-complete even for $k = 2$. The more
   499	interesting version which we use here is that the sizes are encoded in unary so all sizes are
   500	polynomial. In such a case, the \binpack\ problem is known to be solvable in time $t^{\O(k)}$ using
   501	dynamic programming where $t$ is the total size of all items. And it is \cW1-hard with respect to
   502	the parameter $k$~\cite{binpacking}. The similar holds for $\ext(\pint,\fixed)$:
   503	
   504	\begin{proof}[Theorem~\ref{thm:pint_equiv_bin_pack}]
   505	For a given instance of the \binpack\ problem, we can solve it by $\ext(\pint,\fixed)$ in a similar
   506	manner as in the reduction in Theorem~\ref{thm:int_npc}. As $T'$, take a path $P_{(V+1)k}$. As $G$,
   507	take $P_{A_i-2}$ for each $A_i$ and the pre-drawn vertices $v_0,\dots,v_k$ such that $R_{v_i} =
   508	\{p_{(V+1)i}\}$. The rest of the argument is exactly as in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:int_npc}.
   509	
   510	Now, we want to solve $\ext(\pint,\fixed)$ using $2^k$ instances of \genbinpack\ (which is
   511	polynomially equivalent to \binpack), where $k$ is the number of pre-drawn intervals.
   512	
   513	First we deal with located components $C_1 \blt \cdots \blt C_c$. For each component, we have two possible
   514	orderings $\wlt$ and using Lemma~\ref{lem:pint_minspan} we get (at most) two possible smallest
   515	representations which might be differently shifted. In total, we have at most $2^c \le 2^k$ possible
   516	representations keeping $C_1,\dots,C_c$ as small as possible leaving maximal gaps for unlocated
   517	components. We test each of these $2^c$ representations.
   518	
   519	Let $C'_1,\dots,C'_{c'}$ be the unlocated components. For each $C'_i$, we compute $\minspan(C'_i)$
   520	using Lemma~\ref{lem:pint_minspan}. The goal is to place the unlocated components into the $c+1$
   521	gaps between representations of the located components $C_1,\dots,C_c$. We can solve this problem
   522	using \genbinpack\ as follows. We have $k+1$ bins of the volumes equal to the sizes of the gaps
   523	between the representations of $C_1,\dots,C_c$. We have $c'$ items of the sizes $A_i =
   524	\minspan(C'_i)$.
   525	
   526	A solution of \genbinpack\ tells how to place the unlocated components into the $k$ gaps.
   527	If there exists no solution, this specific representation of the located components cannot
   528	be used. We can test all $2^c$ possible representations of the located components. Thus we get the
   529	required weak truth-table reduction.\qed
   530	\end{proof}
   531	
   532	\begin{corollary} \label{cor:pint_param_k}
   533	The problem $\ext(\pint,\fixed)$ is \cW1-hard and belongs to \cXP, solvable in time $n^{\O(k)}$ where
   534	$k$ is the number of pre-drawn intervals.
   535	\end{corollary}
   536	
   537	\begin{proof}
   538	Both claims follow from Theorem~\ref{thm:pint_equiv_bin_pack}.\qed
   539	\end{proof}
   540	
   541	\begin{proposition} \label{prop:ext_int_hard_in_k}
   542	The problems $\ext(\int,\fixed)$ and $\ext(\int,\add)$ are \cW1-hard when parameterized by the
   543	number $k$ of pre-drawn intervals.
   544	\end{proposition}
   545	
   546	\begin{proof}
   547	We modify the reductions of Theorem~\ref{thm:int_npc} and Corollary~\ref{cor:int_add_npc} exactly as
   548	in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:pint_equiv_bin_pack}.\qed
   549	\end{proof}
   550	
   551	\heading{By the Size of the Path.}
   552	We show that the \fixed\ type problems are fixed-parameter tractable with respect to the size of the
   553	path $t$. It is easy to find a solution by a brute-force algorithm:
   554	
   555	\begin{proposition} \label{prop:int_fixed_size_fpt}
   556	For the size $t$ of a path $T'$, the problems $\ext(\pint,\fixed)$ and $\ext(\int,\fixed)$ are
   557	fixed-parameter tractable with the respect to the parameter $t$. They can be solved in time
   558	$\O(n+m+f(t))$ where
   559	$$f(t) = t^{2t^2}.$$
   560	\end{proposition}
   561	
   562	\begin{proof}
   563	In a pruned graph, the vertices have to be represented by pairwise different intervals. There are at
   564	most $t^2$ possible different subpaths of a path with $t$ vertices so the pruned graph can contain
   565	at most $t^2$ vertices; otherwise the extension is clearly not possible. We can test every possible
   566	assignment of the non-pre-drawn vertices to the $t^2$ subpaths, and for each assignment we test
   567	whether we get a correct representation extending $\calR'$.\qed
   568	\end{proof}
     1	\section{Preliminaries} \label{sec:prelim}
     2	
     3	In this section, we describe the notation used in this paper. Also, we deal with two common concepts
     4	of the partial representation extension problems: Located and unlocated components, and groups of
     5	indistinguishable vertices.
     6	
     7	\heading{Notation.}
     8	We consider finite undirected simple graphs, i.e., graphs without loops and multiedges. As usual,
     9	we reserve $n$ for the number of the vertices and $m$ for the number of the edges of the main
    10	considered graph $G$. The set of its vertices is denoted by $V(G)$ and the set of its edges by
    11	$E(G)$. For a vertex $v \in V(G)$, we let $N(v)=\{x \mid vx\in E(G)\}$ denote the \emph{open
    12	neighborhood} of $v$, and $N[v] = N(v)\cup\{v\}$ the \emph{closed neighborhood} of $v$.
    13	
    14	By $P_n$, we denote the path of the length $n$ with $n+1$ vertices. For a tree, we call the vertices
    15	of degree larger than two \emph{branch vertices} and the vertices of degree at most two
    16	\emph{non-branch vertices}, and of course the vertices of degree one are called \emph{leaves}.
    17	
    18	\heading{The Type Lattice.}
    19	The four types \fixed, \sub, \add, and \both\ form the lattice depicted in
    20	Fig.~\ref{fig:type_lattice}. For a type $\frakT$, we denote by $\gen(\frakT,T')$ the set of all
    21	trees $T$ which we can generate from $T'$ using the modifications of the type $\frakT$. In addition,
    22	if $T'$ contains pre-drawn subtrees, the trees in $\gen(\frakT,T')$ contain these (possibly
    23	subdivided) pre-drawn subtrees as well. The ordering of the types given by the lattice has this
    24	property: If $\frakT \le \frakT'$, then $\gen(\frakT,T') \subseteq \gen(\frakT',T')$.
    25	
    26	\begin{figure}[b!]
    27	\centering
    28	\includegraphics[scale=0.85]{introduction4_type_lattice.eps}
    29	\caption{The lattice formed by four types \fixed, \sub, \add, and \both.}
    30	\label{fig:type_lattice}
    31	\end{figure}
    32	
    33	Whether a given instance is solvable depends on the set $\gen(\frakT,T')$; so if this set contains
    34	more trees, it only helps in solving the problem. Let $\frakT \le \frakT'$. If an instance of
    35	$\recog^*$ or \ext\ is solvable for the type $\frakT$, then it is solvable for the type $\frakT'$ as
    36	well.  Equivalently, if it is not solvable for $\frakT'$, it is also not solvable for $\frakT$.
    37	
    38	For the types \add\ and \both\ (and \sub\ for \pint\ and \int), the set $\gen(\frakT,T')$ contains a
    39	tree having an arbitrary tree $T$ as a subtree. Therefore, the $\recog^*$ problem for these types is
    40	equivalent to the standard $\recog$ problem, and we can use the known polynomial-time algorithms.
    41	
    42	\heading{Topology of Components.} The following property works quite generally for many
    43	intersection-defined classes of graphs, and works for all classes studied in this paper. The only
    44	required condition is that the sets $R_v$ are connected subsets of some topological space, for
    45	example $\mathbb R^k$.  (As a negative example, this property does not hold for \emph{$2$-interval
    46	graphs}.  A graph is a $2$-interval graph if each $R_v$ is a union of two closed intervals.) Let $C$
    47	be a connected component of $G$. Then the property is that for each representation $\calR$, the set
    48	$\bigcup_{v \in C} R_v$ is a connected subset of the space, and we call this subset the \emph{area of
    49	$C$}. Clearly, the areas of the components are pairwise disjoint.
    50	
    51	For the classes \pint\ and \int, the areas of the components have to be ordered from left to right.
    52	Let us denote this ordering by $\blt$, so we have $C_1 \blt \cdots \blt C_c$. For different
    53	representations $\calR$, we can have different orderings $\blt$. When no restriction is posed on
    54	$\calR$, it is possible to create a representation in every of the $c!$ possible orderings.
    55	
    56	\heading{Types of Components.}
    57	For the partial representation extension problem, the graph $G$ contains two types of components.  A
    58	component $C$ is called a \emph{located component} if it has at least one vertex pre-drawn, i.e., $C
    59	\cap G'$ is non-empty. A component $C$ is called an \emph{unlocated component} if no interval is
    60	pre-drawn, i.e., $C \cap G' = \emptyset$. For located components, we have a partial information about
    61	their position.  For unlocated components, we are much freer in their placement.
    62	
    63	For the classes of interval graphs, the located components are ordered from left to right. An
    64	obvious necessary condition for an extendible partial representation is that the pre-drawn intervals
    65	of each component appear consecutively in $\calR'$. Indeed, if $C$ and $C'$ are two distinct
    66	components, $u,v \in C$, $w \in C'$ and $R_w$ is between $R_u$ and $R_v$, then the partial
    67	representation is clearly not extendible.  For every representation $\calR$ extending $\calR'$, the
    68	ordering $\blt$ has to extend the ordering $\blt'$ of the located components in $\calR'$.
    69	
    70	For many of the considered problems the unlocated components are irrelevant. For instance for
    71	$\ext(\int,\both)$, we can extend the path $T$ far enough to the right and place the unlocated
    72	components there, without interfering with the partial representation at all. On the other hand, for
    73	problems involving the types \fixed\ and \sub, the space in $T$ is limited and the unlocated
    74	components have to be placed somewhere. In many cases, the existence of unlocated components is not
    75	only used for \cNP-completeness proofs but also necessary for the problems to be \cNP-complete.
    76	
    77	\heading{Indistinguishable Vertices.}
    78	Let $u$ and $v$ be two vertices of $G$ such that $N[u] = N[v]$. These two vertices are called
    79	\emph{indistinguishable} since they can be represented exactly the same, i.e., $R_u = R_v$. (This
    80	is a common property of indistinguishable vertices for all intersection representations). From the
    81	structural point of view, \emph{groups of indistinguishable} vertices are not very interesting. The
    82	goal is to construct a pruned graph where each group is represented by a single vertex. For that, we
    83	need to be little careful since we cannot prune pre-drawn vertices.
    84	
    85	For an arbitrary graph, its groups of indistinguishable vertices can be located in time
    86	$\O(n+m)$~\cite{recog_chordal_graphs}. We prune the graph in the following way. If $u$ and $v$ are
    87	indistinguishable and $u$ is not pre-drawn, we eliminate $u$ from the graph (and for the
    88	representation, we can put $R_u = R_v$). In addition, if two pre-drawn intervals are the same, we
    89	eliminate one of them. The resulting pruned graph has the following property: If
    90	two vertices $u$ and $v$ indistinguishable, they are both pre-drawn and represented by distinct
    91	intervals. For the rest of the paper, we expect that all input graphs are pruned.
    92	
    93	\heading{Maximal Cliques.}
    94	It is well known that subtrees of a tree possess the Helly property, i.e.,  every pairwise
    95	intersecting collection of subtrees has a non-empty intersection (which is again a subtree).  Hence
    96	the following holds true for all classes of graphs considered. If $K$ is a maximal clique of $G$,
    97	the common intersection $R_K = \cap_{u \in K} R_u$ is a subtree of $T$. This subtree $R_K$ is not
    98	intersected by any other $R_v$ for $v \notin K$ (otherwise $K$ would not be a maximal clique). Thus
    99	the subtrees $R_K$ corresponding to different maximal cliques are pairwise disjoint. For example, if
   100	$|T|$ is smaller than the number of maximal cliques of $G$, the graph is clearly not representable
   101	in $T$.
     1	\section{Conclusions} \label{sec:conclusions}
     2	
     3	In this paper, we have considered different problems concerning extending partial representations of
     4	chordal graphs and their three subclasses. One of the main goals of this paper is to stimulate
     5	a future research in this area. Therefore, we conclude with three open problems.
     6	
     7	The first problem concerns the only open case in the table in Fig.~\ref{fig:complexity_table}.
     8	
     9	\begin{problem}
    10	What is the complexity of $\ext(\path,\both)$?
    11	\end{problem}
    12	
    13	Concerning the parameterized complexity, we believe it is useful to first attack problems related to
    14	interval graphs. This allows to develop tools for more complicated chordal graphs. A generalization
    15	of Theorem~\ref{thm:pint_equiv_bin_pack} and Corollary~\ref{cor:pint_param_k} for \int\ seems to be
    16	particularly interesting. The PQ-tree approach seems to be a good starting point.
    17	
    18	\begin{problem}
    19	Does $\ext(\int,\fixed)$ belong to \cXP\ with respect to $k$ where $k$ is the number of pre-drawn
    20	intervals?
    21	\end{problem}
    22	
    23	We present only basic results concerning the parameterized complexity with respect to the parameter
    24	$t$ where $t$ is the size of the tree $T'$. We deal with the type \fixed\ for which the solution is
    25	straightforward. The complexity for the other types \sub, \add, and \both\ remains open.
    26	
    27	\begin{problem}
    28	What is the parameterized complexity of the remaining problems with respect to the parameter $t$
    29	where $t$ is the size of the tree $T'$?
    30	\end{problem}
    31	

