     1	# arXiv 1207.0255 — fixed-path interval-extension generator
     2	
     3	| Profile | Value |
     4	|---|---|
     5	| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
     6	| Native domain | combinatorics |
     7	| Object regime | finite discrete |
     8	| Computational core | exact cover |
     9	| Certificate | matrix certificate |
    10	| Objects shown | fixed host path, partially represented interval graph, pre-drawn singleton subpaths, unlocated path components |
    11	| Intended intuition | invariant: three size bands carry affine copies of one residue-tag set |
    12	| Domain essentiality | **native** |
    13	| Reduction | none in the solver task; instances use the paper's own Theorem 3 gadgets |
    14	
    15	## What the problem is, and whether to trust it
    16	
    17	The source is Klavik, Kratochvil, Otachi and Saitoh, [*Extending Partial
    18	Representations of Subclasses of Chordal Graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.0255).
    19	The solver receives exactly the objects in the paper's `RepExt(INT, Fixed)`
    20	problem: a fixed path, singleton subpaths already assigned to some graph
    21	vertices, and unlocated path components. It returns a `3n × 2` matrix. Row
    22	`i = [g,r]` puts take component `T_i` in gap `g` at left-to-right rank `r`.
    23	The checker expands that row into the canonical closed-subpath representation
    24	of the entire path component and checks exact gap capacities and boundaries.
    25	No floating point, search, planted-answer comparison, or theorem oracle occurs
    26	in `verify`.
    27	
    28	Local correctness evidence is complete: all nine gates pass, including 12/12
    29	planted checks, 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses, four attacks at 0/8, a
    30	successful Track-B reference at 8/8, and 80 relabelling/reflection checks. The
    31	required external four-vendor hardening verdict is **not complete**: on
    32	2026-09-05 the repository-provided OpenRouter key returned HTTP 403 “Key limit
    33	exceeded (total limit)” on every redraw. The script-owned error transcript is
    34	preserved; it is not presented as model failure.
    35	
    36	## Why this is Track B
    37	
    38	Section 1.2 defines the four host-tree modification types; this family uses
    39	`Fixed`. Section 2 proves that connected components occupy disjoint areas and
    40	distinguishes located from unlocated components. Lemma 6 gives
    41	`minspan(P_A)=A`. In Section 3.3, Theorem 3 places split singletons at
    42	`p_(M+1)j`, leaving `n` gaps of exactly `M` vertices, and represents every
    43	3-PARTITION item of size `A_i` by an unlocated take component `P_Ai`. An
    44	extension exists exactly when three take components fill every gap.
    45	
    46	The general problem is NP-complete, but that is not a distributional hardness
    47	claim. This generator therefore does not claim Track A. Its generated lengths
    48	have a promised affine residue encoding. The disclosed reference algorithm
    49	forms every low-band/middle-band pair and then applies the affine selector. It
    50	is `O(n²)` on this distribution, succeeds 8/8, and at shipping examines 1,764
    51	cross-band pairs and performs 2,060 counted exact operations per instance
    52	(0.006174 s total for eight local instances). The compact route derives
    53	`q²=M/12`, reduces the three bands modulo `q`, and aligns their common tags in
    54	at most 254 exact operations. The benchmark asks whether a no-tool solver sees
    55	that invariant instead of carrying out the larger exact-pair table.
    56	
    57	The easy regimes matter. `Recog*(INT,Fixed)` with no partial representation is
    58	linear by Proposition 1. `RepExt(INT,Sub)` is linear by Theorem 2. Section 3.4
    59	notes that making every component located restores a fixed ordering and a
    60	linear test, and Proposition 5 makes bounded host-path size FPT. The generator
    61	keeps many unlocated components, a growing path, pre-drawn splitters, and the
    62	fixed modification type.
    63	
    64	## Worked demo
    65	
